  [1m[38;5;153mformat.js[0m — added [38;5;153mtruncateToWordBoundary(str, max)[39m as a module-private helper, plus a [38;5;153mtypeof options.truncate === [39m
  [38;5;153m'number'[39m block in [38;5;153mformat()[39m between the case transforms and [38;5;153mprefix[39m. The helper cuts at the last whitespace that fits
  the [38;5;153mmax - 3[39m budget, treats whitespace sitting exactly at the cut point as an already-complete word (so [38;5;153m"abc def ghi"[39m
  at 10 keeps [38;5;153m"abc def..."[39m rather than backing up to [38;5;153m"abc..."[39m), and falls back to a hard cut when no word boundary fits.

  [1m[38;5;153mformat.test.js[0m — 11 new [38;5;153mconsole.assert[39m lines in the existing style: under/at/over the limit, boundary cut, a word
  dropped for overflow, the single-long-token fallback, [38;5;153mtruncate[39m at 3/2/0, composition with [38;5;153muppercase[39m, and composition
  with [38;5;153mprefix[39m.

  Two things worth keeping in mind, both as designed and approved:

  - [38;5;153mtruncate[39m bounds the content, not the final result — [38;5;153m{ prefix: "[log] ", truncate: 8 }[39m returns 14 characters. Say the
    word if you want it to bound the whole output instead.
  - The word-boundary cut can spend well under the budget when a long token follows a short one. [38;5;153m"hello [39m
    [38;5;153msupercalifragilisticexpialidocious"[39m at 30 yields [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m. That's inherent to the strategy; the usual fix is a
    "don't back up more than ~60% of the budget" threshold, which I left out as unrequested tuning.

